"input line too long" — my generator produces lines longer than 256 bytes? Let me check. Actually, the issue might be in my generator: `fmt` with style 3 might produce weird long strings, or... wait, more likely: the LineReader has a bug! Let me check: the check `if (buf_.size() > kMaxLineBytes) fail(...)` happens after compaction — but compaction only happens when pos_ > 0. Hmm, wait: the issue is that the buffer accumulates a whole 64KB chunk. After reading a chunk, buf_ contains up to 65536 bytes INCLUDING many lines. If no '\n' is found in the whole buffer... but there should be '\n's. Oh wait — I see the bug: after the first `next()` call, `pos_` points past the first line. On the second call, `buf_.find('\n', pos_)` — if found, we return. Fine. But when we need more data: `buf_.erase(0, pos_); pos_ = 0;` then read a chunk and append → buf_ = remaining partial line + 64KB. Then the check `buf_.size() > 256` → fails because buf_ now contains 64KB of data with newlines in it!

The check is wrong: it should only check the partial line length (up to the end of the buffer if no '\n' exists in the buffer). But the buffer DOES contain newlines — we just haven't searched after appending. The loop re-searches from pos_ = 0 at the top of the next iteration... wait, no: the check `if (buf_.size() > kMaxLineBytes) fail(...)` happens immediately after the append, BEFORE re-searching. So a buffer with a partial line of 10 bytes + a 64KB chunk = 65546 > 256 → fail. That's the bug!

Fix: the check should measure the partial line length, i.e., the distance from pos_ to the first '\n' (or to the end of the buffer if no '\n'). Simplest correct approach: after appending, loop back to the search; only fail if the search finds no '\n' AND buf_.size() - pos_ > kMaxLineBytes. So move the check to right after the "not found" case, before reading more:

```
